u own your career and achieve  your goals all you have to do is to sign  up for a free account and you can get  started with your learning  now the next resource i'd like to  recommend is workshops.edu  this is something i recommended in my  previous video and a lot of you were  really surprised that there's a website  out there where you can find free  self-paced labs created by different  teams internally at aws this is an aws  site where you can find self-paced  workshops ranging from all levels of  difficulty so you can see there's level  300 level 200 level 100 as well and  there's a lot of workshops you can  choose from so for example this aws  networking workshop you can see that it  covers the full spectrum of aws  networking all the way from the basics  of a vpc and subnets to the more  advanced configurations now aws  workshops are a really great resource to  sort of learn by doing so by following  along those workshops you'll be able to  improve your skills in aws and really  learn about how those services work deep  down  okay so the next resource is a video  series called back to basics it's an  ongoing series of videos that explains  examines and decomposes basic cloud  architecture best practices and each  episode is actually hosted by an aws  solutions architect i would recommend  back to basics for anyone looking to  familiarize themselves with  architectural patterns and get an idea  of how adipose services work together to  address common technical problems  the next resource is the aws free tier  and if you don't know already aws once  you sign up for an account gives you 12  months of access to many of our services  for free and these services you can use  them to a limited capacity without  having to pay a single dollar and this  comes in really handy because as you  probably heard me say over and over  again on this channel the best way to  learn aws is by understanding the  theoretical knowledge but more  importantly building and experimenting  and seeing how these services actually  work in the aws console and how all of  these services fit in together so to  make use of the free tier what you can  do is you can create your own aws  account set up a billing alarm to avoid  unnecessary charges and then you can  start to provision resources and  interact with these resources for  example you can see here that amazon ec2  has a free tier that provides 750 hours  per month for certain t2 micro and t3  micro instances i'll put a link in the  description box below that gives you an  overview of the services covered by the  aws free tier okay so by this point of  the video what i've mentioned is  resources that can help you with aws  certifications it can help you build  your skills in aws and it can also help  you understand some of the comment  architectures but i thought i'd mention  some other resources that can help you  understand the cloud computing industry  better and help you go deeper into your  learning once you have developed a basic  understanding of aws services the first  one is aws re invent videos reinvent is  an annual learning conference hosted by  aws for the global cloud computing  community you can watch as many of these  videos as you'd like for free on youtube  and there are some really cool keynote  speeches product announcements and  customer stories aside from re invent  aws has a bunch of other events and  webinars throughout the year for example  aws summit and aws innovate on youtube  you'll be able to find recordings for  some of those sessions too the second  one is abyss white papers there are  hundreds of free technical white papers  technical guides and reference diagrams  available on this website and reading  through them will help you expand your  knowledge of different aws use cases the  next resource is aws ramp up guides i've  recommended this in a previous video and  it's a really good way to find useful  resources grouped by different  categories so you can see by role by  solution or by industry and for example  if i click on one of the guides you can  see that the resources are centralized  and listed out in the document and the  final resource that i wanted to mention  is the aws twitch channel more  specifically a show called the aws power  hour you can watch the recorded episodes  on demand and it's a pretty fun and  engaging way to learn how to build on  aws alright so this brings us to the  very end of this video please give it a  like if you haven't already and  hopefully you found this video helpful  if you have any video suggestions please  leave it down in the comments below and  subscribe to the channel to stay updated  with future videos
 

